    <h1>Mutiny of the Consonants</h1>
    <p class="author">By Njeri of the Woods</p><div class="poem">I love my white friends, my awkward sweet crew

With their sunburns and sandals, and ‘ I brought hummus too!’
They hug like puppies and speak with such glee
While English sits heavy, just bullying me.

They say ‘literally’ twenty seven times,
They rhyme ‘drama’ with ‘llama’ call it good rhymes
They chatter like teapots left boiling too long.
And I'm nodding along, but my brain’s doing wrong.

They mean well- bless their cotton socks
But English feels like fighting ten angry rocks
My tongue wants to jump ship,vowels want sleep
My consonants mutiny, overboard they leap.

One day they'll learn, one day they'll see
That speaking English all day just finishes me.</div>

